d the heavy news coverage of Pritzker's 2028 ambitions reflects candidate speculation rather than evidence about the state's margin.", "watch": "The arrival of state polling, the identity of the Democratic nominee — particularly a Pritzker home-state effect — or a shift in the national environment could move this forecast.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Illinois remains a Democratic-leaning state in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of roughly six points toward Democrats. The 2028 race is open, with the incumbent president term-limited, so neither party gains an incumbency advantage. No general-election polls, expert ratings, or market pricing are available yet, so the forecast rests on the state's underlying partisan fundamentals.\n\nHeadlines so far center on Governor JB Pritzker's possible 2028 presidential run and on down-ballot Illinois contests rather than the presidential margin itself. A home-state Democratic nominee could add to the party's margin, while a strong national Republican environment could narrow it. Until polling arrives, the six-point Democratic lean is the best available guide, with wide uncertainty reflecting the absence of race-specific data.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7608, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.746, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7069, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7035, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7139, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7208, "margin": 6.0}]}